Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your body is telling you it needs rest — a deload week is the right move now.",
  "summary": "This has been a tough period and the data backs up exactly how you're feeling. Strength indices have dropped across every single muscle group — chest, back, legs, shoulders, biceps, and triceps are all down compared to last period — and your subjective experience of aching joints, general fatigue, and declining session quality points clearly to accumulated fatigue and under-recovery. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI and a solid training history, this isn't a sign that something is wrong with you — it's a sign that your body has been working hard and now needs a deliberate recovery stimulus. The good news is that a well-executed deload week almost always results in a performance rebound, and your long-term muscle-building goal is best served by treating recovery as part of the programme, not a break from it.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "Every muscle group has declined in strength index this period. Chest fell from 105 to 99, back from 104 to 98, legs from 104 to 97, shoulders from 103 to 99, biceps from 101 to 100, and triceps from 102 to 100. This is a uniform, system-wide regression — not an isolated plateau on one lift — which strongly suggests the cause is accumulated fatigue rather than a technique or programming issue with any specific movement. Progressive overload is currently impossible when the body is this run down, and pushing through will only deepen the regression."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs account for only 22.4% of total volume, which is on the lower side for a muscle-building goal where the lower body represents the largest muscle mass available. This is worth addressing once recovery is restored."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps sit at just 4% of total volume, which is the lowest share of any group. While biceps receive indirect work from back training, this proportion is worth keeping an eye on for balanced arm development over time."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "A deload is strongly recommended based on the convergence of multiple warning signs: strength indices have regressed across all six tracked muscle groups, the user reports joint aches, general fatigue, and sessions that are progressively getting worse. This pattern is a textbook signal of accumulated systemic fatigue. Continuing at current volume and intensity will deepen the regression and increase injury risk. A structured deload — typically reducing volume by 40–50% and dropping intensity to around 60–70% of normal working weights for one week — will allow the nervous system and connective tissue to recover and set up a strong rebound into the next training block.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Perform a full deload this coming week: keep the same movement patterns but cut your total sets roughly in half (aim for around 25–28 sets total) and use lighter loads at around 60–70% of your usual working weights. The goal is to move well and flush fatigue, not to train hard.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload — this is when the actual recovery happens. Aim for at least 7–8 hours of sleep per night and make sure you are eating enough protein (a general target for muscle building is around 1.6–2.2g per kg of bodyweight, so roughly 115–160g daily at your current weight).",
    "When you return to full training after the deload, gradually rebuild leg volume toward 25–28% of your total weekly sets to better reflect the size and growth potential of the lower body, which supports your muscle-building goal.",
    "Consider logging how you feel day-to-day during the deload and at the start of the next block. Tracking subjective recovery scores alongside your strength index will help identify fatigue accumulation earlier next time, before it reaches this level."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "User experienced a full-system regression in strength across all muscle groups alongside joint pain, fatigue, and declining session quality — a deload was prescribed and the user is entering the next period from a state of accumulated fatigue with the expectation of a performance rebound."
}
